      "name": null,
      "problem": "The mean number of bacteria per milliliter of a liquid is $6.$ What is the probability that there will be no more than $3$ bacteria in one milliliter of the liquid? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that bacteria appear independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bacteria in one milliliter of the liquid. Since the average rate is $6$ bacteria per milliliter, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of bacteria can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that at most $3$ bacteria will be found in one milliliter of the liquid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}{e}^{-6}}{0!}+\\frac{{6}^{1}{e}^{-6}}{1!}+\\frac{{6}^{2}{e}^{-6}}{2!}+\\frac{{6}^{3}{e}^{-6}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.151\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
