      "name": null,
      "problem": "Previous results indicate that, on average, for each batch of transistors, $1$ is defective. What is the probability that in the next batch of transistors, there will be less than $4$ defective transistors? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective transistors occur independently and at a constant average rate.",
      "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of defective transistors in a batch of transistors. Since the average rate is $1$ defective transistors per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of defective transistors can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =1,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(1).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{1}^{x}{e}^{-1}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that in the next batch of transistors, there will be less than $4$ defective transistors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{0}{e}^{-1}}{0!}+\\frac{{1}^{1}{e}^{-1}}{1!}+\\frac{{1}^{2}{e}^{-1}}{2!}+\\frac{{1}^{3}{e}^{-1}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.981\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
